{#-
  Conditional "Codex second opinion" body section for reviewer agents.

  PLAN-codex-second-llm-integration ADR-007: rendered into 3 allow-listed
  reviewer agent bodies (code-reviewer, consensus-arbiter, plan-validator)
  when codex_second_opinion.enabled.

  PLAN-codex-mandatory-second-opinion: the call is now MANDATORY for
  `plan-validator` (MAY→MUST) with a top-level `codex_status`/
  `codex_reconciliation` output contract + anti-boilerplate floor + loud
  warn-and-proceed (ADR-001/002/003 of that PLAN). code-reviewer and
  consensus-arbiter KEEP the opt-in MAY text — their output is a top-level
  JSON array that the two-pass/verifier/consensus pipeline would strip an
  envelope from, so forcing them is deferred to a follow-up PLAN (ADR-004).
  The branch is on `name`; the marker + Bash recipe + prerequisite are shared.

  Whitespace control: {%- ... -%} dashes so the disabled branch is
  byte-zero (ADR-007 P-W1, Jinja env trim_blocks=False).

  Marker `<!-- @hm:codex-second-opinion -->` is a single, distinct token
  used by tests to assert presence/absence precisely.

  Rendered Bash recipe is hermetic by default (ADR-006). The
  `--output-schema` path points at the rendered schema file (ADR-008).
-#}
{%- set _csoo = config.get('codex_second_opinion', {}) if config else {} -%}
{%- if _csoo.get('enabled') and name in _csoo.get('agents', []) %}

<!-- @hm:codex-second-opinion -->

{% if name == 'plan-validator' -%}
## Required: Codex second opinion

You **MUST** invoke Codex CLI as a second-LLM reviewer on **every** run —
this is mandatory whenever `codex_second_opinion.enabled`, not opt-in. Run
the invocation below, then reconcile **every** Codex finding in your output.
{%- else -%}
## Optional: Codex second opinion

You **MAY** invoke Codex CLI as a second-LLM reviewer when you reach a
finding that benefits from cross-model judgment (M-of-N consensus,
tie-break, architectural sanity check). This is opt-in per call — no
ambient Claude self-dispatch.
{%- endif %}

**Invocation contract** (hermetic by default per ADR-006 — `--ignore-user-config`
and `--ignore-rules` strip developer-specific Codex AGENTS.md so the
second opinion is reproducible across machines):

```bash
tmp=$(mktemp)
# Build the prompt body in a tmpfile so adversarial diff content
# (e.g. a bare `PROMPT_END` line) CANNOT terminate a heredoc early
# (security-reviewer P1: heredoc terminator injection via reviewed diff).
prompt_tmp=$(mktemp)
# IMPORTANT: write the question / diff context to "$prompt_tmp" using
# `printf '%s' "$content" > "$prompt_tmp"` — never inline the content in
# this heredoc and never use eval/sh -c on it.
codex exec \
  --sandbox read-only \
  --ask-for-approval never \
  {%- if _csoo.get('hermetic', true) %}
  --ignore-user-config --ignore-rules \
  {%- endif %}
  --json \
  --output-schema "{{ _csoo.get('output_schema_path', '.claude/schemas/codex-finding.schema.json') }}" \
  --output-last-message "$tmp" \
  - < "$prompt_tmp"
status=$?
if [ "$status" -ne 0 ]; then
  echo "[codex second-opinion] skipped: exit=$status (proceeding Claude-only)" >&2
else
  cat "$tmp"  # JSON conforming to the configured output_schema_path
fi
rm -f "$tmp" "$prompt_tmp"
```

{% if name == 'plan-validator' -%}
**Output contract — this EXTENDS the `## Output JSON Schema` above.** Despite
its "Return ONLY this JSON" wording, when Codex is enabled your returned object
**MUST** carry two **additional top-level** keys alongside `overall_assessment`,
`critiques`, and `clean_categories` (NOT nested inside `critiques`):
- `codex_status`: `"invoked"` when the call succeeded, `"skipped"` when it failed.
- `codex_reconciliation`: an array, one entry per Codex finding —
  `{ "codex_finding_ref": "...", "disposition": "accepted" | "rejected" | "duplicate", "reason": "..." }`.
  Empty array `[]` when `codex_status` is `"skipped"`.

**Anti-boilerplate floor** — every entry's `codex_finding_ref` MUST cite the
specific Codex finding it dispositions: its `file:line` (when present) or a
verbatim quote of the Codex finding `message`. A bare `"rejected: n/a"` with
no reference does NOT satisfy the contract — the mandatory call is a forced
*consideration*, not a decorative one. Your `overall_assessment` stays your
own Claude-derived verdict (Codex is input you cannot silently discard, not a
verdict source — preserves ADR-005 of PLAN-codex-second-llm-integration).

**Failure policy** (ADR-003 — warn-and-proceed, made loud): on any non-zero
exit (auth, rate-limit, network, schema rejection), do NOT block. Set
`codex_status: "skipped"`, `codex_skip_reason: "<one-line cause>"`, and
`codex_reconciliation: []`, then proceed with Claude-only analysis. `/hm:plan`
Step 4 surfaces the skip reason to the user.
{%- else -%}
**Output schema**: `{ findings: [{file, line, severity, message, evidence}],
summary, confidence }` — see `{{ _csoo.get('output_schema_path', '.claude/schemas/codex-finding.schema.json') }}`.

**Failure policy** (ADR-003 — warn-and-proceed): on any non-zero exit
(auth, rate-limit, network, schema rejection), log one line to stderr
and continue with Claude-only output. Do NOT block the stage.
{%- endif %}

**Prerequisite**: user must have run `codex login` on their machine.
First call surfaces an auth error if missing.
{%- endif -%}
